## Why This Is Asked
Analyzes streaming-frequency estimation with bounded memory and batch processing.

## Key Concepts
- Misra-Gries heavy hitters; counter trimming; candidate set.
- Trade-offs: accuracy vs memory; exact counting as baseline.
- Batch processing implications on update costs.

## Code Example
```javascript
// Misra-Gries skeleton
class MG {
  constructor(K){ this.K=K; this.map=new Map(); }
  add(x){ if(this.map.has(x)) this.map.set(x,this.map.get(x)+1); else if(this.map.size<this.K) this.map.set(x,1); else { for(let [k,c] of this.map){ this.map.set(k,c-1); } /* remove zeros */ for(let [k,c] of Array.from(this.map)) if(this.map.get(k)<=0) this.map.delete(k); } }
  }
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
Tests understanding of streaming algorithms with bounded memory and error guarantees. Reservoir sampling is a fundamental technique that demonstrates trade-offs between memory, accuracy, and per-element computational cost.

## Key Concepts
- **Reservoir Sampling**: Maintains a uniform random sample of size S from an infinite stream
- **Unbiased Estimator**: (N/S) * sumS has expectation equal to the true sum
- **Relative Error Bound**: Using Chernoff bounds, P(|X - E[X]| > epsilon * E[X]) <= 2 * exp(-S * epsilon^2 / 3), giving S >= (3/epsilon^2) * ln(2/delta)
- **Time Complexity**: O(1) per element (constant-time random number generation and potential replacement)
- **Space Complexity**: O(S) for storing the reservoir

## Derivation
The probability that any given stream element ends up in the final reservoir is S/N. By linearity of expectation, E[sumS] = S/N * (sum of all N values), so E[(N/S) * sumS] = true sum — the estimator is unbiased. The variance of sumS scales as O(N * M^2 / S), and the relative standard deviation is O(sqrt(N/S)), giving the error bounds above.

## Code Example
```javascript
let S = 100;
let N = 0, sumS = 0;
let sample = [];

function process(value) {
  N++;
  if (sample.length < S) {
    sample.push(value);
    sumS += value;
  } else {
    let j = Math.floor(Math.random() * N);
    if (j < S) {
      sumS += value - sample[j];
      sample[j] = value;
    }
  }
}

function estimateSum() {
  return N === 0 ? 0 : (N / S) * sumS;
}
```

## Worked Example
M=100, B=10, S=50. Stream: [5, 12, 7, 23, 8, ...]. After first batch of 5 values (N=5): reservoir = [5,12,7,23,8], sumS = 55, estimate = (5/5)*55 = 55 (exact). After 100 values with sum=2340: reservoir has 50 values summing to ~1170, estimate = (100/50)*1170 = 2340 (close to true). With S=50 and epsilon=0.1, delta ≈ exp(-50*0.01/3) ≈ 0.85 — increase S to 400 for delta=0.01.

## Why This Is Asked
Tests incremental triangle counting under streaming edge additions; evaluates ability to design batched analysis and compare to full recomputation.

## Key Concepts
- Incremental graph algorithms
- Degree-based edge orientation
- Wedge counting and triangle updates
- Amortized analysis
- Space-time trade-offs

## Code Example
```javascript
function addEdge(u,v,adj,deg,tri){
  if (deg[u] > deg[v]) [u,v] = [v,u];
  let cnt = 0;
  for (const w of adj[u]){
    if (adj[v].has(w)) cnt++;
  }
  tri.total += cnt;
  adj[u].add(v); adj[v].add(u);
  deg[u]++; deg[v]++;
}
```

## Further Discussion
This batched approach significantly outperforms rebuilding from scratch, which would require O(n³) time in the worst case for triangle enumeration. The incremental method achieves near-linear preprocessing and maintains efficient updates, making it practical for large-scale dynamic graphs where edges arrive continuously.

## Why This Is Asked
Tests dynamic skyline maintenance with batch updates, including amortized analysis and practical data-structure design.

## Key Concepts
- **2D dominance and Pareto frontier**: A point p dominates q if p.x ≥ q.x and p.y ≥ q.y with at least one strict inequality.
- **Invariant**: Frontier F is sorted by x with strictly increasing y, ensuring no two frontier points dominate each other.
- **Amortized analysis**: Each point is inserted once and removed at most once over n total points.
- **Space**: Uses O(n) space for all historical points plus O(|F|) for the current frontier.

## Data Structure
Use a balanced binary search tree (e.g., TreeMap) keyed by x-coordinate, with y-values stored alongside. This enables O(log |F|) insertion, O(1) access to the rightmost (largest x) point, and O(|F|) in-order traversal.

## Algorithm
1. Sort incoming batch by x-coordinate (O(B log B)).
2. Merge with frontier: for each batch point p, if p.y > current max frontier y, insert p and prune any frontier points with y ≤ p.y.
3. Enumerate frontier via in-order traversal.

## Code Example
```javascript
class Skyline {
  constructor() { this.frontier = new Map(); }

  insertBatch(points) {
    const sorted = points.sort((a, b) => a.x - b.x);
    let maxY = 0;

    for (const p of sorted) {
      if (p.y <= maxY) continue;

      for (const [x, y] of this.frontier) {
        if (y <= p.y) this.frontier.delete(x);
      }

      this.frontier.set(p.x, p.y);
      maxY = p.y;
    }
  }

  getFrontier() {
    return Array.from(this.frontier.entries())
      .sort((a, b) => a[0] - b[0]);
  }
}
```

## Complexity Summary
| Operation | Cost |
|-----------|------|
| Batch insertion | O(B log B + |F|) amortized |
| Enumerate frontier | O(|F|) |
| Space | O(n) |

## Why This Is Asked
Tests an engineer's ability to design incremental statistics with tight per-batch updates and constant-time queries, a realistic analytics constraint.

## Key Concepts
- Maintaining a nonlinear statistic (entropy) via a linearizable surrogate S
- Precomputing k log k and handling zero edges cleanly
- Incremental updates using two counters per move

## Code Example
```javascript
// G[k] = k * Math.log(k), with G[0] = 0
function processBatch(batch, freq, G, S, n) {
  for (const {from,to} of batch) {
    const fFrom = freq[from];
    freq[from] = fFrom - 1;
    S.value += G[fFrom - 1] - G[fFrom];
    const fTo = freq[to];
    freq[to] = fTo + 1;
    S.value += G[fTo + 1] - G[fTo];
  }
  return Math.log(n) - S.value / n;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you adapt if m changes over time or updates include new categories?
- How do you handle numerical stability for very large n and skewed distributions?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests the ability to reason about incremental data structures and Bloom filter trade-offs for query pruning in streaming text.

## Key Concepts
- Incremental suffix automaton with amortized O(1) update per character
- Bloom filters for O(1) query pruning per pattern, per batch
- Cache behavior: larger B reduces SAM update frequency but increases Bloom filter staleness and memory pressure
- Space: each block's Bloom filter costs O(B) bits; false positives introduce extra SAM verifications

## Code Example
```javascript
// Extend SAM with a block of text
function extendSAM(sam, block) {
  for (const char of block) {
    sam.extend(char);
  }
}

// Build Bloom filter for a block's substrings
function buildBlockBloom(block, size) {
  const bloom = new BloomFilter(size, 0.01);
  for (let i = 0; i < block.length; i++) {
    for (let len = 1; len <= block.length - i; len++) {
      bloom.add(block.slice(i, i + len));
    }
  }
  return bloom;
}

// Query patterns against accumulated text
function queryPatterns(sam, bloomFilters, patterns) {
  return patterns.map(p => {
    const mightMatch = bloomFilters.every(bf => bf.test(p));
    return mightMatch && sam.contains(p);
  });
}
```

